# Less-terse STE (chat and new markdown)

Write to Ries in ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English. Use the same voice in `.md` you write. Do not sweep old files just to make them longer.

Still STE: active voice, simple tenses, one word for one thing, no slang. Keep file paths, flags, prices, and error text exact, in backticks.

Prefer one idea per sentence. A short second clause is allowed when it stops a telegram. There is no hard 20-word cap. Do not cut `because` or `so` just to shrink a sentence. Split a long sentence; never compress one into jargon. Complete sentences. Explain why, not only the headline. Do not write status as a table of fragments when a short paragraph is clearer.

```
BAD (telegram):
- Hook green.
- D263 stands.
- Cap gone.

GOOD:
The STE gate still runs. `ste-sentence` now reports only, so a longer sentence does not fail the commit. D263 still governs STE voice. D304 retires the 20-word cap.
```

HermesHost committed `.md` still keeps the 6-sentence paragraph ratchet, the idiom and term-drift checks, and the qualifier guard.

## Pit of failure, pit of success

This plain voice **is** STE for Ries, not a second style. Start with the human picture. Then give the exact token in backticks.

Name the pit of failure: where we left the agent in the dark. Then name the pit of success: what we hand him so he cannot make that mistake. Say where *we* made the flow go dark. Do not only blame the model.

Do not open with wrap jargon (`generation`, digest, `_fold_aim_peek`, same-hash) when a photo or sticker picture will do.

```
BAD (compiler-speak):
A same-hash peek in `_fold_aim_peek` omitted `snapshot`, so `_aim_handle_ok` kept `@e16` valid after the Samenvatting remount.

GOOD (this is STE doing its job):
After the Samenvatting click we sent "success" and a short hash. We did not send the new photo of the page. Barney kept the old stickers (`@e16`) from chat. Those stickers were already dead. Halt stopped the second stab. The pit of success is: after a hop, hand him the new photo and retire the old stickers in the same result.
```
